Several points:

 -- Kernighan is probable the best technical writer I know;
    anything he's (co-)authored is eminently readable.  In fact,
    in my opinion, the main reason for C's enormous success is
    the fact that Kernighan was the author of its main textbook.

 -- At once time (in the mid 1980's), I and a collegue were
    called on to review books for possible purchace for the
    technical library at Siemens.  What we'd do to start with
    was open to a page at random, and see if it contained more
    than one or two errors.  In something like 20 or 30 books,
    all but one did.  (That one was by Guy Steele.  Another
    reference.)  Be very, very careful about using any random
    book.

 -- The ACCU (http://www.accu.org/) has a lot of very good book
    reviews.  You might want to check them out.

 -- And finally, it you really want to develope software, you'll
    need to know a lot more than just the language.  Design
    methodologies, algorithms, etc., even if you're only doing
    it for fun.  It's too much to take on in one go, but don't
    forget it once you've learned the basics of a language.

    [...]
> Finally, some advice:
>
> 1) Don't ever say void main(void) because it's wrong. Say int main(void).

He won't if he's using gcc; gcc treats it as an error.  (At
least, g++ does in C++.)

> 2) Don't rely on learning by trial-and-error. A C program which is
> incorrect may still compile and execute fine! (One example is a C
> program which uses void main(void).) You also /need/ a good textbook,
> such as K&R2 mentioned earlier, in order to know when you are writing C
> which is correct.

That's probably true for any language, but especially so with
C/C++, because of the enormous amount of undefined behavior in
the languages.

